.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt ACCESS 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm access ,
.Nm faccessat
.Nd check file existence and permissions
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn access "const char *path" "int mode"
.In fcntl.h
.Ft int
.Fn faccessat "int dirfd" "const char *path" "int mode" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn access
checks whether
.Fa path
exists and grants the permissions requested by
.Fa mode .
.Dv F_OK
(zero) checks existence only.
The other modes may be combined with bitwise OR:
.Bl -tag -width W_OK
.It Dv R_OK
Read permission.
.It Dv W_OK
Write permission.
.It Dv X_OK
Execute permission for a file, or search permission for a directory.
.El
.Pp
The kernel uses the caller's real user and group IDs and supplementary
groups for both directory search and the target's permissions.
The owner bits apply when the real user ID matches the owner;
otherwise the group bits apply when a group matches, and the other bits
apply when none does.
Real user ID 0 bypasses the mode-bit check, except that a regular file
on KRFFS or another disk file system needs at least one execute bit for
.Dv X_OK .
For example, an existing
.Pa /proc/PID/stat
grants
.Dv F_OK
and
.Dv R_OK
but denies
.Dv W_OK
to a caller whose real user ID is nonzero.
After the permission check succeeds,
.Dv W_OK
on a read-only mount other than the KRFFS boot root gives
.Er EROFS .
.Pp
Relative paths are resolved from the current working directory.
.Fn faccessat
instead resolves them from the directory descriptor
.Fa dirfd ,
or from the working directory when
.Fa dirfd
is
.Dv AT_FDCWD .
An absolute path ignores
.Fa dirfd .
The descriptor is resolved to a path and does not bypass ancestor
search permissions.
Symbolic links are followed.
.Pp
The kernel calls are 33 for
.Fn access
and 307 for
.Fn faccessat .
Call 307 takes only
.Fa dirfd ,
.Fa path
and
.Fa mode .
Pass zero as
.Fa flags
to use it directly through musl.
Musl rejects flags other than
.Dv AT_EACCESS .
With that flag and equal real and effective IDs it uses the same call.
With differing IDs its helper requires the unimplemented
.Fn clone
call and fails with
.Er EBUSY .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return 0 when all requested checks succeed.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The raw kernel calls return negative error numbers on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default-suite probe
.Pa tests/permprobe.c
checks that an unprivileged caller can read
.Pa /hello.txt
but receives
.Er EACCES
when testing it with
.Dv W_OK .
.Pa tests/searchprobe.c
checks real-ID permission decisions with unequal real and effective IDs.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ENAMETOOLONG
.It Er EACCES
Search permission is denied on a path component, or the target denies a
requested permission.
.It Er EBADF
A relative path uses an invalid or stale
.Fa dirfd .
.It Er EBUSY
Path resolution enters a mount being mounted or unmounted.
Musl also uses this error when its
.Dv AT_EACCESS
helper cannot complete.
.It Er EFAULT
The path cannot be read from user memory.
.It Er EINVAL
.Fa mode
has bits other than
.Dv R_OK ,
.Dv W_OK
and
.Dv X_OK ,
or musl rejects
.Fa flags .
.It Er EIO
Path lookup or metadata access fails, including access to a removed device.
.It Er ELOOP
Path resolution would follow more than 40 symbolic links.
.It Er ENAMETOOLONG
The supplied path or its joined absolute form exceeds 255 bytes, a
directory descriptor's path cannot be reconstructed within its buffer,
a component exceeds 255 bytes, or symbolic-link expansion exceeds
511 bytes.
.It Er ENOENT
The path is empty, a component is missing, or a symbolic link has an
empty target.
.It Er ENOMEM
Path lookup cannot allocate a file-system node.
.It Er ENOTDIR
A path component used as a directory is not a directory, or a relative
path uses a nondirectory
.Fa dirfd .
.It Er EROFS
.Dv W_OK
was requested on a read-only mount other than the KRFFS boot root
after its mode check succeeded.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chmod 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr stat 2
